  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cold front sinks south Thursday and Friday, bringing \ngradually improving conditions with high pressure overhead Saturday\nand the first half of Sunday.\n\nThe stationary front that has lingered across the region the\nlast few days will slowly sink south as a cold front Thursday \nand Friday, allowing gradually improving conditions to filter\nin. Zonal flow aloft will keep Thursday largely dry, though\ncooler temperatures and lower humidity will not arrive until\nFriday and especially Saturday. A few showers will remain \npossible Friday night as the cold front finally shifts far south\nenough to allow surface high pressure to be overhead Saturday \nand into early Sunday. The forecast Saturday looks to be the\ndriest, coolest, and least humid day through the weekend with\nhighs in the upper 70s to mid 80s and dew points only reaching\nthe low to mid 60s. High pressure will not last, however, and\nshift offshore Sunday, bringing return flow back across the\nregion, allowing temperatures to creep back up into the mid to\nupper 80s and the humidity to return. The morning will be\nlargely dry with chances for rain returning by the afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather returns Sunday afternoon into\nMonday.\n\nThe next storm system arrives Sunday afternoon into Monday as a\nsurface low moves across the Great Lakes, Ohio Valley, and\neventually the Mid Atlantic, bringing the chance for showers and\nthunderstorms. Global guidance shows PWAT values surging back\ninto the 2\" range, increasing the potential for heavy downpours\nwithin any thunderstorms that develop. As a result, WPC does\nhave much of the Northeast and Mid Atlantic in a Marginal risk\nin the Excessive Rainfall Outlook. Furthermore, some modest \ninstability may be in place to support a severe potential. That \nsaid, the overall synoptic level forcing is limited with the \nmain driving force being a weak upper level trough. At this \npoint, it remains too early to lock down any specifics for\nsevere and flash flood, but we will need to monitor the\npotential over the next several days.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Spotty minor tidal flooding possible during the\nThursday evening high tide along the Atlantic coast, Delaware \nBay, and tidal Delaware River.\n\nWith the New Moon having occurred yesterday, astronomical \ntides for this evening's high tide cycle will be near minor \nflooding thresholds for many places along the Atlantic coasts of\nNew Jersey and Delware, Delaware Bay, and the tidal Delaware \nRiver, though tides are expected to begin trending downward as\nwe move away from the New Moon. As of the latest forecast, only\nspotty minor flooding is possible with this evening's high \ntide.\n\nTidal flooding is not currently forecast for the Eastern Shore of \nthe Chesapeake Bay.",
